{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Arise, walk through the land, etc.</b> In the chapter [dealing with] one who sells produce (Bava Batra 100a), and this is its language: It was taught [in a Baraita]: One who walks through [land] lengthwise and breadthwise acquires the place of his walking — these are the words of Rabbi Eliezer. And the Sages say: He does not acquire until he takes possession [through a formal act of ḥazakah]. What is the reason of Rabbi Eliezer? For it is written: ״קוּם הִתְהַלֵּךְ etc. לְאָרְכָּהּ״ etc. And the Rabbis [explain]: there [in that verse] it was because of the belovedness of Abraham etc., so that it would be easy to conquer before his children — end quote. Now according to the opinion of Rabbi Eliezer, even though [Abraham] took possession through the stretching out of the land and its drawing near before him, as we explained regarding the verse ״כִּי אֶת כָּל הָאָרֶץ״ etc., nevertheless, from the fact that the verse says <b>Arise, walk through</b> etc., it comes to teach Torah law that walking constitutes an act of possession [ḥazakah]. But according to the opinion of the Sages, that it was because of the belovedness etc., the possession of the land as a merit for Abraham was secured by a Scriptural decree on high, and we do not learn from this verse [for general halakhic principles], because the belovedness of Abraham is its intent."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:07.449054848Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}